1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,960
What extra information lets a reflection find
an object outside the screen? We compare scene

2
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:12,072
representations and ray acceleration, follow
spatial and temporal reuse of limited samples,

3
00:00:12,072 --> 00:00:16,962
then separate Nanite's geometry selection from
Lumen's lighting updates.

4
00:00:16,962 --> 00:00:20,400
We begin with three representations of the
same wall.

5
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:24,809
Screen-space reflections read visible color
and depth.

6
00:00:24,809 --> 00:00:29,651
Off-screen information requires another
representation. Camera depth,

7
00:00:29,651 --> 00:00:35,492
world-space distance and voxel lighting may
all appear as grids but store different data.

8
00:00:35,492 --> 00:00:40,132
A depth image summarizes the front surface at
each pixel. Its contents change with the

9
00:00:40,132 --> 00:00:44,772
camera. Repeated reads cannot fully recover
hidden surfaces that were never stored.

10
00:00:44,772 --> 00:00:50,745
A distance field stores distance from spatial
points to a nearby surface,

11
00:00:50,745 --> 00:00:55,368
possibly with a sign distinguishing inside and
outside.

12
00:00:55,368 --> 00:01:00,762
Our wall diagram shows distance layers rather
than camera pixels.

13
00:01:00,762 --> 00:01:07,898
Voxels partition volume. They may store
occupancy, material or directional lighting.

14
00:01:07,898 --> 00:01:14,262
The term voxel alone does not specify a
reflection or illumination algorithm.

15
00:01:14,262 --> 00:01:19,547
Ambient occlusion summarizes visibility,
whereas global illumination includes

16
00:01:19,547 --> 00:01:25,132
transported light and color. Darkening a
corner does not fully compute color bleeding.

17
00:01:25,132 --> 00:01:28,312
Distance-field AO can query world-space data.

18
00:01:28,312 --> 00:01:33,002
Screen-space GI uses color and depth but
retains screen limitations.

19
00:01:33,002 --> 00:01:37,613
Moving a hidden object off-screen tests that
information boundary.

20
00:01:37,613 --> 00:01:44,461
Coarse distance fields or voxels can lose thin
walls or merge nearby surfaces.

21
00:01:44,461 --> 00:01:48,922
Coverage, detail and update cost must be
balanced.

22
00:01:48,922 --> 00:01:52,867
Rays can query scene triangles beyond the
screen,

23
00:01:52,867 --> 00:01:59,440
but that does not make the work cheap. We
first need a way to skip unrelated geometry.

24
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:05,960
A ray is an origin plus a direction multiplied
by a parameter.

25
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:13,692
Before testing every triangle, we can test
boxes enclosing groups of triangles.

26
00:02:13,692 --> 00:02:19,811
A BVH organizes bounding volumes
hierarchically. Missing a parent box allows

27
00:02:19,811 --> 00:02:27,042
its descendants to be skipped. We distinguish
visited and rejected branches in the diagram.

28
00:02:27,042 --> 00:02:35,223
An axis-aligned box can be tested using ray
intervals between the two bounds of each axis.

29
00:02:35,223 --> 00:02:41,222
Without a common interval across all axes, the
ray misses the box.

30
00:02:41,222 --> 00:02:44,804
Our example uses intervals of two to six,
three to five,

31
00:02:44,804 --> 00:02:47,917
and four to seven. Their overlap is four to
five.

32
00:02:47,917 --> 00:02:53,522
Replacing one interval with eight to nine
removes the overlap and rejects the branch.

33
00:02:53,522 --> 00:03:01,819
A box hit does not guarantee a geometry hit.
Bounds conservatively retain candidates;

34
00:03:01,819 --> 00:03:06,592
leaf geometry still needs intersection
testing.

35
00:03:06,592 --> 00:03:10,707
Closest-hit and visibility queries have
different goals.

36
00:03:10,707 --> 00:03:15,234
One needs the nearest valid surface, while
another can terminate

37
00:03:15,234 --> 00:03:19,842
after finding an appropriate blocker. Their
costs need not match.

38
00:03:19,842 --> 00:03:26,092
Poor bounds can group unrelated objects and
leave excessive candidate work. Building a

39
00:03:26,092 --> 00:03:32,342
better hierarchy also costs time. Traversal
quality and construction cost are linked.

40
00:03:32,342 --> 00:03:37,553
Hardware ray tracing in 2018 accelerated
portions of intersection work.

41
00:03:37,553 --> 00:03:43,520
It did not make traversal, material evaluation
and scene updates universally free.

42
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:51,865
A mesh can appear in multiple scene instances.
Geometry-level and scene-instance acceleration

43
00:03:51,865 --> 00:03:58,002
structures separate those responsibilities,
commonly called BLAS and TLAS.

44
00:03:58,002 --> 00:04:05,245
Moving an instance differs from deforming its
triangles. The required structure

45
00:04:05,245 --> 00:04:12,592
updates change, and update versus rebuild
conditions depend on the engine and API.

46
00:04:12,592 --> 00:04:19,297
A cheap update does not always preserve
optimal traversal. Deformation can reduce

47
00:04:19,297 --> 00:04:25,522
bound quality, trading lower update cost for
more expensive future queries.

48
00:04:25,522 --> 00:04:31,378
Hybrid rendering combines a rasterized base
with selected ray-traced effects such as

49
00:04:31,378 --> 00:04:37,152
shadows or reflections. Different tasks can
use different methods within one frame.

50
00:04:37,152 --> 00:04:42,501
Battlefield V's initial DXR reflections
illustrate that hybrid approach.

51
00:04:42,501 --> 00:04:46,346
They are distinct from later full path-tracing
modes,

52
00:04:46,346 --> 00:04:50,942
and game release dates should be separated
from feature updates.

53
00:04:50,942 --> 00:04:57,345
Access to off-screen lights does not remove
noise from sparse rough-reflection samples.

54
00:04:57,345 --> 00:05:02,400
The next question is how to produce a stable
estimate with limited work.

55
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:08,860
When many directions or paths contribute,
samples can estimate their combined effect.

56
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:12,505
The estimator must account for selection
probability,

57
00:05:12,505 --> 00:05:15,452
not simply average arbitrary chosen values.

58
00:05:15,452 --> 00:05:20,642
Two equally weighted teaching values, two and
eight, average to five.

59
00:05:20,642 --> 00:05:26,359
Sampling eight more often without compensation
biases the estimate upward.

60
00:05:26,359 --> 00:05:30,492
Better selection still requires appropriate
weights.

61
00:05:30,492 --> 00:05:37,729
Monte Carlo estimators divide contributions by
sampling density. Rare, large contributions

62
00:05:37,729 --> 00:05:44,512
can have large weights. Distribution design
therefore matters alongside sample count.

63
00:05:44,512 --> 00:05:49,730
Under independent sampling and finite
variance, standard error scales inversely with

64
00:05:49,730 --> 00:05:54,948
the square root of sample count. Halving it
requires roughly four times as many samples.

65
00:05:54,948 --> 00:05:58,332
Correlated or filtered samples need separate
analysis.

66
00:05:58,332 --> 00:06:03,089
Accumulation can add information in a static
scene. In motion,

67
00:06:03,089 --> 00:06:07,396
the same pixel coordinate can belong to
another surface.

68
00:06:07,396 --> 00:06:12,153
Image coordinates and surface correspondence
are different.

69
00:06:12,153 --> 00:06:18,145
Two separated frame planes help visualize
correspondence. Object motion and

70
00:06:18,145 --> 00:06:24,320
camera motion determine which previous
location might provide usable history.

71
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:29,606
DLSS 2, introduced in 2020, combines a
lower-resolution current image,

72
00:06:29,606 --> 00:06:34,845
motion information and temporal feedback to
produce a higher-resolution output.

73
00:06:34,845 --> 00:06:38,812
Its approach differs from the earliest DLSS
implementations.

74
00:06:38,812 --> 00:06:42,007
Reconstruction consumes engine-rendered
information.

75
00:06:42,007 --> 00:06:44,811
It is not the same stage as casting additional
rays.

76
00:06:44,811 --> 00:06:50,093
Savings from lower internal resolution must be
considered together with reconstruction cost.

77
00:06:50,093 --> 00:06:54,640
Halving both image dimensions gives one
quarter as many pixels.

78
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:59,524
That does not imply one quarter of total game
cost, since simulation,

79
00:06:59,524 --> 00:07:03,903
geometry, memory and reconstruction may scale
differently.

80
00:07:03,903 --> 00:07:07,531
Motion vectors help locate previous
correspondence.

81
00:07:07,531 --> 00:07:11,396
Their direction, units and jitter convention
must agree.

82
00:07:11,396 --> 00:07:16,523
A single global image shift cannot represent
independently moving objects.

83
00:07:16,523 --> 00:07:20,919
Newly revealed surfaces may lack history and
require greater reliance on current

84
00:07:20,919 --> 00:07:25,060
information. Depth or surface changes help
reject invalid correspondence;

85
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:27,672
more accumulation is not automatically better.

86
00:07:27,672 --> 00:07:33,289
Thin moving lines, small lettering and
disocclusion boundaries are useful tests.

87
00:07:33,289 --> 00:07:38,182
Still sharpness and temporal stability remain
separate observations.

88
00:07:38,182 --> 00:07:40,731
We keep the discussion at renderer inputs,

89
00:07:40,731 --> 00:07:44,696
outputs and failure conditions rather than
neural architecture.

90
00:07:44,696 --> 00:07:49,440
Missing information is not guaranteed to be
recovered correctly in every case.

91
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:56,599
DDGI updates spatial probes with rays and lets
surfaces query the resulting irradiance field.

92
00:07:56,599 --> 00:08:02,153
This can avoid independently tracing long
indirect-light paths for every pixel.

93
00:08:02,153 --> 00:08:08,629
Probes are world-space locations rather than
camera pixels. Their directional ray

94
00:08:08,629 --> 00:08:14,923
results can update with lighting and serve
multiple surfaces as the camera moves.

95
00:08:14,923 --> 00:08:21,280
Unconditionally blending nearby probes can
leak light across walls.

96
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:27,853
Distance and visibility information are needed
to constrain the query.

97
00:08:27,853 --> 00:08:33,458
Our diagram contrasts distance-only blending
with occlusion-aware weighting across a wall.

98
00:08:33,458 --> 00:08:38,643
The example explains the query, without
claiming a universal implementation formula.

99
00:08:38,643 --> 00:08:45,186
Wider probe spacing smooths small features.
Denser probes cost storage and updates,

100
00:08:45,186 --> 00:08:50,002
while partial updates add convergence-time
considerations.

101
00:08:50,002 --> 00:08:55,478
Dynamic updates do not imply instantaneous
perfect results. Moving lights

102
00:08:55,478 --> 00:09:01,040
and wall boundaries help separate latency,
leakage and computational cost.

103
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:09,574
ReSTIR's 2020 paper addressed dynamic direct
lighting by reusing candidates over

104
00:09:09,574 --> 00:09:17,053
space and time. Later GI and path-tracing
variants are distinct developments.

105
00:09:17,053 --> 00:09:20,968
A reservoir keeps a selected candidate and
summary information

106
00:09:20,968 --> 00:09:25,318
rather than all candidates. Its weighted
selection is probabilistic,

107
00:09:25,318 --> 00:09:28,653
not an instruction to always keep the
brightest light.

108
00:09:28,653 --> 00:09:33,311
In our example, weights one and three give a
cumulative weight of four.

109
00:09:33,311 --> 00:09:38,285
The second candidate replaces the first with
probability three quarters,

110
00:09:38,285 --> 00:09:41,523
leaving the first with probability one
quarter.

111
00:09:41,523 --> 00:09:45,309
This illustrates weighted selection only. A
final lighting estimator

112
00:09:45,309 --> 00:09:48,969
also accounts for proposal distributions and
represented samples.

113
00:09:48,969 --> 00:09:52,502
The replacement probability alone is not the
complete estimator.

114
00:09:52,502 --> 00:10:00,184
Neighboring and historical candidates broaden
the search. Their usefulness can change with

115
00:10:00,184 --> 00:10:07,963
surface position, orientation and visibility,
so reuse requires appropriate reevaluation.

116
00:10:07,963 --> 00:10:13,293
Closing a door can occlude a previously useful
light. Rechecking current visibility

117
00:10:13,293 --> 00:10:18,252
explains why reusing a candidate differs from
copying an old brightness value.

118
00:10:18,252 --> 00:10:24,582
ReSTIR concerns sample selection and reuse;
denoising concerns stabilizing

119
00:10:24,582 --> 00:10:30,720
evaluated results. They can work together
without being interchangeable.

120
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:37,889
Efficient lighting does not eliminate geometry
and visibility work. Frustum culling rejects

121
00:10:37,889 --> 00:10:43,693
objects outside the view, while occlusion
culling targets hidden objects within it.

122
00:10:43,693 --> 00:10:49,150
Being inside the view volume does not
guarantee visibility.

123
00:10:49,150 --> 00:10:56,533
A wall can hide an in-frustum object. The two
tests address different conditions.

124
00:10:56,533 --> 00:11:02,872
Early depth testing can avoid shading occluded
samples when pipeline conditions permit.

125
00:11:02,872 --> 00:11:07,459
Depth writes or shader side effects can change
those conditions;

126
00:11:07,459 --> 00:11:10,963
not every hidden fragment is automatically
free.

127
00:11:10,963 --> 00:11:16,523
A hierarchical depth buffer summarizes regions
for conservative occlusion tests.

128
00:11:16,523 --> 00:11:21,066
Its reduction and comparison rules must match
the depth convention,

129
00:11:21,066 --> 00:11:23,573
including reversed depth where used.

130
00:11:23,573 --> 00:11:30,346
Previous-frame depth can become stale when
doors open or the camera moves quickly.

131
00:11:30,346 --> 00:11:36,733
Conservative handling prevents newly visible
objects from being discarded.

132
00:11:36,733 --> 00:11:42,653
These culling methods resemble BVH rejection
in spirit but act at different pipeline

133
00:11:42,653 --> 00:11:48,080
stages. Identify which query they accelerate
and which data they pass onward.

134
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:55,525
Mesh shaders provide a pipeline for generating
and processing grouped geometry. That

135
00:11:55,525 --> 00:12:02,343
role is separate from indirect illumination
and can support geometry-work reduction.

136
00:12:02,343 --> 00:12:09,922
Variable Rate Shading changes how frequently
shading is evaluated in selected regions.

137
00:12:09,922 --> 00:12:15,683
Shading frequency and output pixel count are
separate dimensions.

138
00:12:15,683 --> 00:12:21,473
A two-by-two teaching block can share one
shading evaluation. Platform support and

139
00:12:21,473 --> 00:12:27,183
pipeline behavior still matter, and this is
not a promise of fourfold performance.

140
00:12:27,183 --> 00:12:35,230
Virtual texturing manages residency of needed
pages rather than keeping every texture region

141
00:12:35,230 --> 00:12:42,573
resident. It concerns data availability, not
learned generation of texture content.

142
00:12:42,573 --> 00:12:48,457
Sampler feedback can report texture demand.
Knowing the demand does not make

143
00:12:48,457 --> 00:12:54,613
data immediately available; streaming latency
and residency remain relevant.

144
00:12:54,613 --> 00:13:00,471
Geometry groups, shading rate and texture
pages target different resources.

145
00:13:00,471 --> 00:13:06,240
Nanite, VRS and DLSS should not be collapsed
into one resolution technology.

146
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:11,893
Nanite's 2020 reveal introduced a workflow for
selecting and supplying needed

147
00:13:11,893 --> 00:13:16,605
detail from dense geometry. We distinguish the
2021 technical description

148
00:13:16,605 --> 00:13:21,023
from later engine features. It does not make
unlimited triangles free.

149
00:13:21,023 --> 00:13:24,114
Geometry is partitioned into clusters,
grouped,

150
00:13:24,114 --> 00:13:28,089
simplified and repartitioned into multiple
representations.

151
00:13:28,089 --> 00:13:32,433
This supports finer-grained selection than a
few whole-object LODs.

152
00:13:32,433 --> 00:13:36,599
The need for fine geometry depends on
projected error. The same world-space

153
00:13:36,599 --> 00:13:41,483
deviation appears smaller at greater distance,
changing the required representation.

154
00:13:41,483 --> 00:13:47,250
Our illustrative projection estimate is focal
length times world-space

155
00:13:47,250 --> 00:13:52,552
error divided by distance. Doubling distance
halves the estimate.

156
00:13:52,552 --> 00:13:56,273
This is not the complete Nanite selection
rule.

157
00:13:56,273 --> 00:14:02,624
Independently simplified neighbors can form
cracks. Group boundaries and repartitioning

158
00:14:02,624 --> 00:14:08,813
maintain consistency. Triangle reduction alone
does not solve hierarchy construction.

159
00:14:08,813 --> 00:14:13,051
Selecting clusters and making their data
resident are different jobs.

160
00:14:13,051 --> 00:14:17,220
Rapid camera motion exposes both visibility
and streaming behavior;

161
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:20,823
triangle visualization alone does not measure
memory cost.

162
00:14:20,823 --> 00:14:26,383
Nanite is a geometry system, not a lighting
solution. We now connect it to Lumen,

163
00:14:26,383 --> 00:14:31,280
which updates indirect illumination and
reflections over the scene.

164
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:39,830
Lumen combines tracing methods with cached
lighting. We separate finding a hit,

165
00:14:39,830 --> 00:14:46,393
storing surface information and querying the
final illumination.

166
00:14:46,393 --> 00:14:49,663
Screen tracing uses detailed visible surfaces.

167
00:14:49,663 --> 00:14:54,528
Misses or unsuitable screen-space paths can
fall back to scene tracing,

168
00:14:54,528 --> 00:14:59,473
preserving screen-space benefits without
relying on them exclusively.

169
00:14:59,473 --> 00:15:06,261
Software and hardware tracing can use
different scene representations.

170
00:15:06,261 --> 00:15:14,473
Distance-field and triangle-based modes
require explicit version and setting labels.

171
00:15:14,473 --> 00:15:19,254
The Surface Cache provides reusable surface
information at ray hits.

172
00:15:19,254 --> 00:15:22,982
Cards relate to capturing meshes from multiple
views;

173
00:15:22,982 --> 00:15:26,873
they are not simply replacements for the
original walls.

174
00:15:26,873 --> 00:15:32,786
Our cutaway separates walls, capture cards and
lighting caches in depth.

175
00:15:32,786 --> 00:15:38,033
Hit detection and cached-light lookup receive
distinct arrows.

176
00:15:38,033 --> 00:15:42,588
The reviewed initial UE5 reveal includes Lumen
off/on views showing

177
00:15:42,588 --> 00:15:47,615
changed indirect lighting in the cave. Those
pixels demonstrate an effect,

178
00:15:47,615 --> 00:15:51,543
not every internal pass or a universal
performance result.

179
00:15:51,543 --> 00:15:57,533
Nanite can accelerate mesh captures used by
lighting caches. That connection does not

180
00:15:57,533 --> 00:16:03,360
make it the definition of Lumen; geometry
supply supports a separate lighting task.

181
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:11,091
A light change can affect direct lighting and
cached indirect lighting at different rates.

182
00:16:11,091 --> 00:16:14,915
Partial updates require budgets and
priorities,

183
00:16:14,915 --> 00:16:18,193
making cache age a quality consideration.

184
00:16:18,193 --> 00:16:23,461
Newly exposed surfaces or major lighting
changes reduce history reliability.

185
00:16:23,461 --> 00:16:27,157
Stability and responsiveness can conflict, so
trails,

186
00:16:27,157 --> 00:16:30,853
noise and convergence speed must be examined
together.

187
00:16:30,853 --> 00:16:34,838
Cache coverage can fail on complex surfaces or
interiors.

188
00:16:34,838 --> 00:16:40,288
Debug views help identify missing coverage
before changing mesh organization

189
00:16:40,288 --> 00:16:45,413
or representation; brightness controls alone
cannot supply absent data.

190
00:16:45,413 --> 00:16:48,762
Thin walls and small bright objects challenge

191
00:16:48,762 --> 00:16:52,456
representation resolution and sampling
budgets.

192
00:16:52,456 --> 00:16:58,383
A technology name alone does not establish
that leakage or instability is solved.

193
00:16:58,383 --> 00:17:03,295
Separate fixed-camera lighting changes from
fixed-lighting camera movement.

194
00:17:03,295 --> 00:17:07,125
This distinguishes screen-trace changes from
scene updates,

195
00:17:07,125 --> 00:17:10,593
while exposure and post-processing remain
controlled.

196
00:17:10,593 --> 00:17:16,546
Dynamic illumination does not imply complete
transport solved anew every frame.

197
00:17:16,546 --> 00:17:22,240
Lumen combines tracing, storage and
distributed updates as an engine system.

198
00:17:22,960 --> 00:17:27,736
We separated scene representations, ray
acceleration, sample reuse,

199
00:17:27,736 --> 00:17:33,148
geometry selection and lighting caches. BVHs
reduce intersection candidates,

200
00:17:33,148 --> 00:17:38,243
while probes and reservoirs manage different
forms of reused information.

201
00:17:38,243 --> 00:17:42,893
Nanite selects needed geometry; Lumen traces
and updates illumination.

202
00:17:42,893 --> 00:17:47,168
Sharing an engine does not make their
responsibilities identical.

203
00:17:47,168 --> 00:17:51,893
Evaluate information coverage, work budget and
update latency together.

204
00:17:51,893 --> 00:17:57,902
Next we connect shadow pages, path tracing,
reconstruction and post-processing.

205
00:17:57,902 --> 00:18:01,936
We distinguish rendered frames from generated
display

206
00:18:01,936 --> 00:18:06,400
frames and follow renderer inputs and outputs
through DLSS 5.
